      "message": "I may still be missing a user, but as far as I can see both callers at the top of the chain pass the slice that `vma_slice_offset()` gives the address.\n\n`remap_vmalloc_range_partial()` (aosp/4367949) passes `offset_in_page(kaddr) \u003e\u003e PAGE_SHIFT_4KB`, and `remap_vmalloc_range()` adds `vma_slice_off(vma)` slices to its kernel-page-aligned `kaddr`, which then advances with `uaddr`. Its other callers, in `fs/proc/vmcore.c`, aren\u0027t built in GKI.\n\n`p3s_vm_map_pages()` (aosp/4368010) maps slice `(pos \u003e\u003e PAGE_SHIFT_4KB) \u0026 P3S_SLICE_MASK` of `pos \u003d vma_file_offset(vma) + off`, which is `vma_slice_offset()` of `vm_start + off`. The `vm_map_pages_zero()` case only differed when `vm_slice_off !\u003d 0`, which aosp/4368056 PS17 now clears there.\n\nThe BPF ring buffer layout from the PS11 message can\u0027t get here anymore either, since aosp/4367950 refuses BPF map `mmap()` in 4KB processes.\n\nWould it be OK to drop this patch and have those two call `vm_insert_page()` and `vm_map_pages()`? aosp/4367611 and aosp/4367612 give explicitly inserted slices as their reason, so they might get much smaller too.",
